---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const categories = [
  {
    id: 'gorilla',
    label: 'Gorilla Trekking',
    icon: 'M12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm-2 15l-5-5 1.41-1.41L10 14.17l7.59-7.59L19 8l-9 9z',
    faqs: [
      {
        q: 'How much does a Uganda gorilla trekking permit cost?',
        a: 'A Uganda gorilla trekking permit costs USD 800 per person for foreign non-residents. Foreign residents pay USD 700, and East African citizens pay USD 80. The permit covers your park entry fee for the day, a Uganda Wildlife Authority ranger guide, and 24-hour armed security escort. During the low season months of April, May, and November, the permit is discounted to USD 600 for foreign non-residents.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Is a gorilla trekking permit guaranteed to see gorillas?',
        a: 'Yes — the Uganda Wildlife Authority habituates gorilla families specifically for tourism, meaning the gorillas are accustomed to human presence. Your ranger guide will track the family the morning of your trek using GPS data from the previous evening. Sightings are not legally guaranteed, but the success rate is consistently above 99%. Bwindi currently has 19 habituated gorilla families available for visitors.'
      },
      {
        q: 'How far in advance should I book a gorilla permit?',
        a: 'We strongly recommend booking at least 3 to 6 months in advance, especially for travel during the peak dry seasons: June to September and December to February. Permits are limited to a maximum of 8 visitors per gorilla family per day, and the most popular families sell out quickly. Once you confirm your permit date with us, we arrange all logistics around it — accommodation, transport, and activities.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the minimum age for gorilla trekking in Uganda?',
        a: 'The minimum age is 15 years, set by the Uganda Wildlife Authority. This rule protects the gorillas from childhood illnesses and ensures all participants can meet the physical demands of the trek. Children under 15 cannot trek even if they hold a paid permit, and no refund is issued in that case. There is no upper age limit — seniors and elderly travelers trek regularly with the option of a sedan chair carrier for additional support.'
      },
      {
        q: 'How long does gorilla trekking take?',
        a: 'Trekking time varies from 30 minutes to 8 hours depending on where the gorilla family has moved overnight. The average trek takes 2 to 4 hours through the forest. Once you find the gorillas, you are allowed exactly 1 hour with them. The rangers are strict about this time limit to minimize stress on the animals. The total day out — including transfer from your lodge, briefing, and trekking — typically takes 6 to 9 hours.'
      },
      {
        q: 'How physically difficult is gorilla trekking?',
        a: 'Gorilla trekking ranges from moderate to strenuous depending on the day. Trails pass through dense rainforest on steep, often muddy slopes at altitudes between 1,500m and 2,500m above sea level. A reasonable level of fitness is recommended — you should be comfortable walking for several hours on uneven terrain. Porters are available for hire at the trailhead for USD 15–20 and carry your daypack as well as assist you on difficult sections. Walking sticks are provided free.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What are the rules during gorilla trekking?',
        a: 'The Uganda Wildlife Authority enforces strict rules: maintain a minimum distance of 7 metres from the gorillas at all times; no flash photography; no eating, drinking or smoking in the gorillas\' presence; keep voices low and calm; do not trek if you have a cold, flu, or any transmissible illness; a maximum of 8 people per gorilla family per day; and the 1-hour time limit is strictly enforced. These rules protect the gorillas and ensure future generations can experience them.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the gorilla habituation experience?',
        a: 'The Gorilla Habituation Experience (GHEX) is available in the Rushaga sector of Bwindi and allows you to spend 4 hours with a gorilla family that is still being habituated to human presence. It costs USD 1,500 per person and gives a much more immersive experience than the standard 1-hour trek. This is ideal for photographers and wildlife enthusiasts who want extended time with the gorillas.'
      },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'planning',
    label: 'Planning Your Safari',
    icon: 'M17 12h-5v5h5v-5zM16 1v2H8V1H6v2H5c-1.11 0-1.99.9-1.99 2L3 19c0 1.1.89 2 2 2h14c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2V5c0-1.1-.9-2-2-2h-1V1h-2zm3 18H5V8h14v11z',
    faqs: [
      {
        q: 'What is the best time of year to visit Uganda for a safari?',
        a: 'Uganda can be visited year-round — gorilla trekking is possible in all months. The best conditions are during the two dry seasons: June to September and December to February. Trails are drier, forest walks are easier, and wildlife viewing is excellent. The wet seasons (March to May and October to November) bring lush green landscapes and fewer crowds, with discounted permit prices, but trails are muddier and harder. Even in the wet season, rain usually falls in short afternoon bursts rather than all day.'
      },
      {
        q: 'How many days do I need for a Uganda safari?',
        a: 'A minimum of 3 days is needed for a single-activity safari such as gorilla trekking or Murchison Falls. For a complete Uganda experience that includes gorilla trekking, chimpanzee tracking, a game drive, and a boat cruise, plan for 7 to 10 days. Our most popular itinerary is 7 days, covering Bwindi, Kibale Forest, and Queen Elizabeth National Park. If you want to combine Uganda with Kenya or Rwanda, allow 10 to 14 days.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Is Uganda safe for tourists?',
        a: 'Yes — Uganda is one of the safest countries in East Africa for tourists. The national parks and tourist areas are well-patrolled by Uganda Wildlife Authority rangers. Kampala is a welcoming capital city. The main safety precautions are standard travel awareness: do not display valuables publicly, use recommended transport providers, and follow your guide\'s advice at all times. All L\'okawa tours include experienced local guides who know the terrain and ensure your safety throughout.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Do I need a visa to visit Uganda?',
        a: 'Most nationalities require a visa to enter Uganda, which can be obtained online via the Uganda e-Visa portal before travel. The standard tourist visa costs USD 50 and covers a single entry for 90 days. An East Africa Tourist Visa (USD 100) grants entry to Uganda, Rwanda, and Kenya on a single permit — excellent value if you plan to visit multiple countries. We recommend applying at least 2 weeks before travel. Let us know your nationality and we will advise on the exact requirements.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What vaccinations do I need for Uganda?',
        a: 'Yellow Fever vaccination is mandatory for entry into Uganda and you must carry your Yellow Card certificate. Recommended vaccinations include Hepatitis A and B, Typhoid, Tetanus, Meningococcal Meningitis, and Rabies (if doing extended outdoor activities). Malaria prophylaxis is strongly recommended for all visitors — consult your GP or travel health clinic at least 4 to 6 weeks before departure. Anti-malarial medication, mosquito repellent (DEET-based), and long-sleeved clothing for evenings are all advisable.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Uganda vs Rwanda — which is better for gorilla trekking?',
        a: 'Both are excellent but serve different traveler profiles. Uganda (USD 800 permit) offers better value, more habituated gorilla families (19 vs 12 in Rwanda), longer trekking terrain for those who enjoy the adventure, and the option to combine gorillas with chimp tracking and game drives. Rwanda (USD 1,500 permit) is better for those who prefer luxury lodges, shorter treks to the gorillas, and a more polished tourism infrastructure. Most first-timers on a budget choose Uganda; returning travelers or those wanting luxury often choose Rwanda.'
      },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'booking',
    label: 'Booking & Payments',
    icon: 'M20 4H4c-1.11 0-2 .89-2 2v12c0 1.11.89 2 2 2h16c1.11 0 2-.89 2-2V6c0-1.11-.89-2-2-2zm0 14H4v-6h16v6zm0-10H4V6h16v2z',
    faqs: [
      {
        q: 'How do I book a safari with L\'okawa Tours?',
        a: 'Simply send us an enquiry via our Contact page or WhatsApp (+256 761 512 966) with your preferred dates, number of travelers, and the destinations or activities you are interested in. We will respond within 24 hours with a tailored itinerary and quotation. Once you are happy with the proposal, a 30% deposit confirms your booking and we immediately secure your gorilla permits and accommodation. The balance is due 30 days before your arrival date.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What deposit is required to confirm a booking?',
        a: 'A 30% deposit of the total tour cost is required to confirm your safari. This deposit covers the gorilla and chimpanzee permit fees, which must be paid in full to the Uganda Wildlife Authority at the time of booking as they are non-refundable. The remaining 70% is due 30 days before your departure. For last-minute bookings made within 30 days of departure, full payment is required at the time of booking.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What payment methods do you accept?',
        a: 'We accept international bank transfer (USD preferred), Western Union, and mobile money for clients based in Uganda. Bank transfer details are provided with your booking confirmation. All tour prices are quoted in US Dollars. We do not currently accept credit cards directly, but we can advise on secure third-party options if needed. Please note that bank transfer fees are the responsibility of the client.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the cancellation and refund policy?',
        a: 'Cancellations more than 60 days before departure incur a 30% cancellation fee of the total tour cost. Cancellations between 30 and 60 days before departure incur a 50% fee. Cancellations within 30 days of departure are non-refundable. Gorilla and chimpanzee permits are always non-refundable once purchased from Uganda Wildlife Authority — this is a government policy we cannot override. We strongly recommend purchasing comprehensive travel insurance that covers safari cancellation.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What is included in the tour price?',
        a: 'All L\'okawa tour packages include: all meals as specified in the itinerary (breakfast, lunch, and dinner), ground transport in a private 4x4 safari vehicle with roof hatch, park entrance fees, an English-speaking professional driver-guide, bottled drinking water throughout, and boat cruise fees where listed. Gorilla and chimpanzee permits are included in packages where those activities are listed. Excluded unless specified: international and domestic flights, visa fees, travel insurance, alcoholic beverages, laundry, gratuities for guides (recommended USD 10–15 per day), and optional activities.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Do you offer custom and private safari packages?',
        a: 'Absolutely. The majority of our safaris are tailored to each client\'s specific dates, budget, group size, and interests. If you cannot find a package that matches exactly what you want, contact us and we will design a bespoke itinerary. We can combine any destinations across Uganda, Kenya, Tanzania, and Rwanda, adjust the pace and accommodation level, and add special experiences such as gorilla habituation, birding with a specialist guide, or a community walk. There is no extra charge for custom planning.'
      },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'packing',
    label: 'Packing & Preparation',
    icon: 'M20 6h-2.18c.07-.44.18-.88.18-1 0-2.21-1.79-4-4-4s-4 1.79-4 4c0 .12.11.56.18 1H8c-1.1 0-2 .9-2 2v12c0 1.1.9 2 2 2h12c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2V8c0-1.1-.9-2-2-2zm-6-3c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2s-.9 2-2 2-2-.9-2-2 .9-2 2-2zm0 13l-4-4 1.41-1.41L14 13.17l5.59-5.59L21 9l-7 7z',
    faqs: [
      {
        q: 'What should I wear for gorilla trekking?',
        a: 'Wear long-sleeved shirts and long trousers in neutral or khaki colours (avoid bright colours and camouflage). Long sleeves protect against stinging nettles and insects in the forest. Sturdy, waterproof hiking boots with ankle support are essential — the terrain is steep and muddy. Wear thick garden gloves to grip vegetation and protect hands. Bring a lightweight rain jacket as the forest is often wet regardless of season. Gaiters are useful to keep mud out of your boots. Avoid perfume, cologne, or strongly scented products.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What should I pack for a general East Africa safari?',
        a: 'Pack light — most safari vehicles and light aircraft have luggage weight limits of 15kg. Essentials include: neutral-coloured safari clothes (2–3 outfits), a warm fleece or jacket for early morning game drives, a wide-brimmed hat, high-SPF sunscreen, a DEET-based insect repellent, personal medications and anti-malarials, a small first-aid kit, a quality camera (the wildlife is extraordinary), binoculars, a phone with offline maps, a universal power adapter, and sufficient USD cash for tips and purchases. Branded safari clothing is not required.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Do I need travel insurance?',
        a: 'Yes — we require all clients to have valid comprehensive travel insurance before their safari begins. Your policy should cover: emergency medical evacuation (critical in remote areas far from hospitals), trip cancellation and curtailment, loss of permits and pre-paid activities, baggage loss, and personal liability. Safari-specific or adventure travel insurance policies are available from providers such as World Nomads, Battleface, or your home country insurer. We can help you understand what to look for. Traveling without adequate insurance is a significant risk.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Is it safe to drink the water in Uganda?',
        a: 'Do not drink tap water in Uganda. All L\'okawa safari vehicles carry unlimited bottled drinking water throughout your tour. At lodges and camps, boiled or filtered water is provided. Brush your teeth with bottled water. When buying water in towns, check the seal is intact. Avoid ice in drinks at basic restaurants. Fruit that you peel yourself (bananas, oranges) is generally safe. We brief all clients on food and water safety before departure.'
      },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'wildlife',
    label: 'Wildlife & Destinations',
    icon: 'M12 2C8.13 2 5 5.13 5 9c0 5.25 7 13 7 13s7-7.75 7-13c0-3.87-3.13-7-7-7zm0 9.5c-1.38 0-2.5-1.12-2.5-2.5s1.12-2.5 2.5-2.5 2.5 1.12 2.5 2.5-1.12 2.5-2.5 2.5z',
    faqs: [
      {
        q: 'Which national park is best for seeing the Big Five in Uganda?',
        a: 'Queen Elizabeth National Park is Uganda\'s best park for general wildlife viewing, with lions (including the famous tree-climbing lions of the Ishasha sector), elephants, buffaloes, leopards, and hippos. The Kazinga Channel boat cruise offers remarkable close-up encounters with hippos and a huge variety of birds. Murchison Falls National Park in the north is excellent for giraffes, elephants, Nile crocodiles, and the Shoebill Stork — one of Africa\'s most sought-after birds. Uganda lacks rhinos in the wild, but white rhinos can be tracked on foot at the Ziwa Rhino Sanctuary.'
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I see chimpanzees in Uganda?',
        a: 'Yes — Uganda has the highest concentration of chimpanzees in East Africa. Kibale Forest National Park near Fort Portal is the best place, with over 1,500 chimps in habituated communities that you can track with a guide. A chimpanzee tracking permit costs USD 200 per person. Chimp tracking is also possible at Kyambura Gorge (Queen Elizabeth NP), Budongo Forest (Murchison Falls NP), Kalinzu Forest, and Ngamba Island Sanctuary on Lake Victoria. Many of our packages combine chimp and gorilla trekking.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What wildlife can I see on a Kenyan safari?',
        a: 'Kenya is home to iconic savannah wildlife. The Masai Mara National Reserve offers the best lion, cheetah, and leopard viewing in Africa, as well as the Great Wildebeest Migration (July to October) when over 1.5 million wildebeest cross from the Serengeti. Amboseli National Park is famous for large elephant herds set against Mount Kilimanjaro. Lake Nakuru hosts flamingos, white and black rhinos, and Rothschild\'s giraffes. Samburu has unique northern species: reticulated giraffe, Grevy\'s zebra, and the gerenuk.'
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the Great Wildebeest Migration and when does it happen?',
        a: 'The Great Wildebeest Migration is the largest mass movement of land animals on Earth — over 1.5 million wildebeest and hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles circle annually between the Serengeti (Tanzania) and the Masai Mara (Kenya). The dramatic Mara River crossings — where crocodiles wait for wildebeest — happen between July and October in the Masai Mara. From January to March the calving season in Tanzania\'s Serengeti is equally spectacular. Our Kenya safari packages are timed to catch the best of the migration.'
      },
    ]
  },
];

const schema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": categories.flatMap(cat =>
    cat.faqs.map(f => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": f.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a }
    }))
  )
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gorilla Trekking & Safari FAQ | L'okawa Tours Uganda"
  description="Answers to the most common questions about Uganda gorilla trekking, safari costs, permits, packing, safety, best time to visit and booking with L'okawa Tours."
  schema={schema}
>

<!-- ── Page hero ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="faq-hero">
  <div class="faq-hero__inner">
    <span class="kicker">Before you book</span>
    <h1 class="faq-hero__h1">Safari &amp; Gorilla Trekking FAQ</h1>
    <p class="faq-hero__sub">
      Everything you need to know about gorilla trekking permits, safari costs, packing, booking and what to expect on your East Africa adventure.
    </p>
    <!-- Category quick-jump pills -->
    <div class="faq-cats">
      {categories.map(cat => (
        <a href={`#${cat.id}`} class="faq-cat-pill">{cat.label}</a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FAQ sections ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="faq-body">
  <div class="container">
    {categories.map(cat => (
      <section id={cat.id} class="faq-section">
        <!-- Section heading -->
        <div class="faq-section__head">
          <div class="faq-section__icon">
            <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="var(--color-amber)" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d={cat.icon}/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h2 class="faq-section__title">{cat.label}</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Accordion items -->
        <div class="faq-list">
          {cat.faqs.map((item, i) => (
            <details class="faq-item" name={`faq-${cat.id}`}>
              <summary class="faq-item__q">
                <span>{item.q}</span>
                <svg class="faq-item__chevron" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="faq-item__a">
                <p>{item.a}</p>
              </div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── Still have questions CTA ───────────────────────────── -->
<section class="faq-cta">
  <div class="container faq-cta__inner">
    <div class="faq-cta__text">
      <h2 class="faq-cta__heading">Still have a question?</h2>
      <p class="faq-cta__sub">Our team responds within 24 hours. We are happy to walk you through every detail of your trip, from permits to packing lists.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-group">
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn--primary btn--lg">Send Us a Message</a>
      <a href="https://wa.me/256761512966" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn--secondary btn--lg">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
/* ── Hero ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.faq-hero {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--color-forest) 0%, var(--color-forest-mid) 100%);
  padding: 80px var(--section-pad-x) 72px;
  text-align: center;
}
.faq-hero__inner { max-width: 760px; margin-inline: auto; }
.faq-hero .kicker { color: var(--color-gold); display: block; margin-bottom: 12px; }
.faq-hero__h1 {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  font-size: var(--text-5xl);
  color: var(--color-white);
  line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  margin-bottom: 18px;
}
.faq-hero__sub {
  font-size: var(--text-lg);
  color: var(--color-cream);
  line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  max-width: 600px;
  margin-inline: auto;
  margin-bottom: 32px;
  opacity: .92;
}

/* Category pills */
.faq-cats {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 10px;
  justify-content: center;
}
.faq-cat-pill {
  background: rgba(255,255,255,.12);
  color: var(--color-cream);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.25);
  border-radius: var(--radius-full);
  padding: 8px 18px;
  font-size: var(--text-sm);
  font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  transition: background var(--transition-fast), border-color var(--transition-fast), color var(--transition-fast);
}
.faq-cat-pill:hover {
  background: var(--color-amber);
  border-color: var(--color-amber);
  color: var(--color-forest-deep);
}

/* ── Body ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.faq-body { padding: 72px 0 80px; background: var(--color-bg); }

.faq-section {
  margin-bottom: 64px;
  scroll-margin-top: 100px; /* offset for sticky header */
}
.faq-section:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

/* Section heading */
.faq-section__head {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 14px;
  margin-bottom: 24px;
  padding-bottom: 16px;
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-card-border);
}
.faq-section__icon {
  width: 44px;
  height: 44px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: var(--color-parchment);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
.faq-section__title {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  font-size: var(--text-3xl);
  color: var(--color-forest);
}

/* Accordion list */
.faq-list {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 2px;
}

.faq-item {
  border: 1px solid var(--color-card-border);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--color-white);
}
.faq-item[open] { border-color: var(--color-amber); }

.faq-item__q {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16px;
  padding: 18px 22px;
  font-family: var(--font-body);
  font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  font-size: var(--text-base);
  color: var(--color-forest);
  cursor: pointer;
  list-style: none;
  transition: background var(--transition-fast);
  line-height: var(--leading-normal);
}
.faq-item__q::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
.faq-item__q:hover { background: var(--color-parchment); }
.faq-item[open] .faq-item__q {
  background: var(--color-parchment);
  color: var(--color-amber);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-card-border);
}
.faq-item__chevron {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  color: var(--color-amber);
  transition: transform var(--transition-base);
}
.faq-item[open] .faq-item__chevron { transform: rotate(180deg); }

.faq-item__a {
  padding: 18px 22px 22px;
  font-size: var(--text-base);
  line-height: 1.75;
  color: var(--color-text-body);
}

/* ── CTA banner ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
.faq-cta {
  background: var(--color-parchment);
  padding: 72px var(--section-pad-x);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-card-border);
}
.faq-cta__inner {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 28px;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
.faq-cta__text { flex: 1 1 400px; }
.faq-cta__heading {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  font-size: var(--text-4xl);
  color: var(--color-forest);
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}
.faq-cta__sub {
  font-size: var(--text-md);
  color: var(--color-text-body);
  line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
}

/* Responsive */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .faq-hero__h1 { font-size: var(--text-4xl); }
  .faq-cta__inner { flex-direction: column; text-align: center; }
  .faq-cta__inner .btn-group { justify-content: center; }
}
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .faq-hero { padding: 60px 24px 56px; }
  .faq-section__title { font-size: var(--text-2xl); }
  .faq-item__q { padding: 14px 16px; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .faq-item__a { padding: 14px 16px 18px; }
}
</style>
